      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats around a round table, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Total Number of Arrangements**: \n   Since the table is round, the total number of ways to arrange 9 people is calculated by considering the circular permutations, which is given by \\((9-1)!\\) or simply \\(8!\\).\n\n   \\[\n   8! = 40,320\n   \\]\n\n2. **Combinations for Math Majors**:\n   First, we choose which 4 out of the 9 seats will be occupied by the math majors. This can be done in \\(\\binom{9}{4}\\) ways.\n\n   \\[\n   \\binom{9}{4} = \\frac{9!}{4!(9-4)!} = \\frac{9!}{4!5!} = 126\n   \\]\n\n3. **Math Majors Sit Consecutively**:\n   Once the 4 seats for math majors are chosen, we consider the total number of seating arrangements for these 4 math majors as \\(2^4 - 1\\) since each packet of two can be arranged in \\(2\\) ways and subtract one to avoid double-counting the cases where they are all together (i.e., the arrangements where all math majors sit consecutively). \n\n   \\[\n   2^4 - 1 = 16 - 1 = 15\n   \\]\n\n4. **Biology Majors and Physics Majors**:\n   The remaining 5 seats must be filled by the 3 physics majors and the 2 biology major/biology majors. The number of ways to arrange these 5 people is given by \\(5!\\):\n\n   \\[\n   5! = 120\n   \\]\n\n5. **Probability Calculation**:\n   The probability is the ratio of the favorable outcomes (math majors in consecutive positions and the rest followed) to the total outcomes:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total outcomes}} = \\frac{126 \\times 15 \\times 120}{8!} = \\frac{126 \\times 15 \\times 120}{40,320}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Simplification**:\n   \n   Calculate the numerator:\n\n   \\[\n   126 \\times 15 \\times 120 = 226800\n   \\]\n\n   Now, divide by the denominator \\((8!)\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{226800}{40,320} = 5.625\n   \\]\n\n   This simplifies to:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\frac{7}{30}}\n   \\]",
