      "text": "Since all the other\npeople are free to\nsitings (either math,\nphysics, or\nbiology), let this\ngiventhiseating positions, D will be\nthe minimum number of pigeonholes. Let\n2\n\nThe pigeonhole principle states that you have a certain number of “pigeonholes” and a certain number of “pigeons,” and if you place more than the minimum number of pigeons than holes, then you must have a minimum of one pigeonhole containing multiple pigeons.\nNote: there is only 1 big t in there, but I did not call anyone a “minihepton.”\nThere are 24 possible seating arrangements for the persons not including the math majors. You can find this by assuming that Person A will sit in a particular seat, then necessarily Person B will sit in another seat, and so on, giving an order of $ABCD$\nPermutations leave the order of persons unchangeable, so there are only two permutations for that group: $ACDB$ or $CDBA$.\nThere are 4 more possible seating arrangements by the math majors: $T^4$ or $VT^4$, $T^5$ or $VT^5$, 4 more for the physics majors: $T^6$ or $VT^6$, and finally 6 more for the biology majors: $T^7$ or $VT^7$. This means that for total of $4+4+6+6=20$\nSo there are 20!/20 combinations out of total direct seating arrangements. 20! is the distance between two person-not in a consecutive seat, and 20 is the same for the other 4. Now You can multiply fractions and get: 5./200 so $1/40$. $1/40$ of 24 is 6. So the probability is $6/(24)=1/4$ So the answer is 1/4.\n\ncontrast with the answer of 2/7.\nA few things I didn't quite catch: what is $\\boxed{D}$, what is the pigeonhole principle, and how can there be \"7 of each\" math majors together?\n\"I too had forgotten the question.\"\nI see beginning helpers. This is a good video on permutations, taking into consideration around a table with a resulting formula that matches the question sheet.\nMaybe the best way of solving is to visually / manually find (permutations)~1 seat arrangement (There are 20! ways to arrange 24, though that was given anyway)-----then cross out the other seats all with the hands in fingers(since who sits what? Assume the original arrangement ), finally, Box the 4 options you don't need in a key way (indistinguishable pairs next to each other, is that, or something else???)---------Meaning----------------->find the solutions.\n1. 6 X 20! / 24\n2. Similar to the 1. it's 1/4.\nWe are dividing 20! by 4! (4 math majors) because the other 20! arrangements of the group would include permutations. Once all the 4 math majors are seated, we want to determine if the other individuals can be seated until independent of the rest. Simply by looking at the seating in the problem, we can see that the number of persons of others each own 6 seats each (evaluate the table and 'divide' the seats). Just multiply it is pretty straightforward to compute. If you don't, Ive provided a way of solving it.\nI mean, \"There are 20! ways to arrange 24, though that was given anyway\". I'm guessing some of the options for the total number permutation is given.",
